  "text": "umably, the polynomial $x^4 + 3x^3 - 5x^2 + 9x - 12$ can be factored into a product of two quadratic polynomials with integer coefficients. To do this, we can use the fact that a quartic polynomial with integer coefficients can be factored into two quadratic polynomials with integer coefficients if and only if it has integer roots.\n\nTo find the integer roots, we can use the Rational Root Theorem, which states that if a polynomial has integer coefficients, then any rational root of the polynomial must be of the form $\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient.\n\nIn this case, the constant term is $-12$ and the leading coefficient is $1$. Therefore, any rational root must be of the form $\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p$ is a factor of $-12$ and $q$ is a factor of $1$. The factors of $-12$ are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 4, \\pm 6, \\pm 12$ and the factors of $1$ are $\\pm 1$. Therefore, the possible rational roots are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 4, \\pm 6, \\pm 12$.\n\nWe can test each of these possible roots by substituting them into the polynomial and checking if the result is $0$. If we find a root, then we can factor the polynomial by dividing it by the corresponding quadratic polynomial with integer coefficients.\n\nFor example, if we find that $x = 2$ is a root, then we can factor the polynomial by dividing it by $x - 2$. This gives us the quadratic polynomial $x^2 + 5x - 6$. We can then factor this quadratic polynomial into two linear polynomials with integer coefficients. This gives us the quadratic polynomial $x^2 + 5x - 6 = (x - 1)(x + 6)$.\n\nTherefore, we can factor the polynomial $x^4 + 3x^3 - 5x^2 + 9x - 12$ into the product of two quadratic polynomials with integer coefficients as follows:\n\n$$x^4 + 3x^3 - 5x^2 + 9x - 12 = (x - 2)(x^3 + 5x^2 - 6x + 6)$$\n\nWe can then factor the cubic polynomial $x^3 + 5x^2 - 6x + 6$ into two linear polynomials with integer coefficients. This gives us the quadratic polynomial $x^3 + 5x^2 - 6x + 6 = (x - 1)(x^2 + 6x - 6)$.\n\nTherefore, we can factor the polynomial $x^4 + 3x^3 - 5x^2 + 9x - 12$ into the product of two quadratic polynomials with integer coefficients as follows:\n\n$$x^4 + 3x^3 - 5x^2 + 9x - 12 = (x - 2)(x - 1)(x^2 + 6x - 6)$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n$$\\boxed{(x - 2)(x - 1)(x^2 + 6x - 6)}$$",
